<div style="width: 100%; padding: 20px; background-color: #f0f8ff; border: 2px solid #1a5276; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 5px;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Healing Plants of Panama</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> Sáblia Is Helpful for Burns.</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Follow extended speech on familiar topics. Interactive: Ensure mutual understanding in group interactions through clarification and confirmation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow spoken explanations about herbal remedies (e.g., "How do we prepare it"). Can clarify understanding of instructions about plant uses (e.g., "Can you repeat how to use the plant?"). Can interpret peer feedback on how to use healing plants by summarizing key points (e.g., "Juan Carlos said that guanabana (soursop) helps with digestion.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0eaf2; color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0eaf2; color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0eaf2; color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays pictures of common healing plants in Panama (e.g., sábila/aloe vera, hierba de limón/lemongrass, hojas de menta/mint leaves, guanábana/soursop, jengibre/ginger). Students are asked to briefly share what they know about any of them, or if their families use them. The teacher asks, "What do you do if you get a small burn?" prompting initial ideas. (<i>Listening Focus: Students listen to the teacher's questions and peers' initial responses.</i>)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces key vocabulary orally by pointing to pictures: "This is <b>sábila</b>, also known as <b>aloe vera</b>. It has a special <b>leaf</b>. This plant is very <b>useful</b> as a <b>remedy</b> for <b>burns</b>." The teacher models the pronunciation of compound nouns like "aloe vera," "mint leaves," and "healing plants," emphasizing the first syllable. The teacher also models the pragmatic functions "You <b>should</b> use sábila for burns" and "It's <b>used to soothe</b> the skin." (<i>Listening Focus: Students listen to the correct pronunciation and target phrases.</i>)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding with questions like:
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of sábila is a 'leaf'?" (pointing to the plant)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If something 'soothes' your skin, does it make it feel better or worse?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time]</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "The Healing Plants of Panama".</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students listen to a short audio segment (approximately 1.5-2 minutes) from a local radio interview or a simulated conversation between two Panamanians discussing natural remedies for common ailments, specifically focusing on how to use sábila (aloe vera) for burns and mentioning other plants like ginger for infections, and mint leaves for headaches. The genre is a conversational dialogue, with an informal and informative register. (<i>Listening Focus: Students listen for overall understanding and specific details.</i>)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The learner can understand brief oral descriptions of common natural remedies and their uses, and follow simple instructions related to their application.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to the audio twice.
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions:</b>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What are the speakers mainly talking about?" (e.g., food, weather, home remedies)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which plant do they talk about the most for skin problems?"</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Statements:</b> Students decide if statements about the audio are True or False.
                        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Sábila is used to treat headaches." (False)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"You should apply the sábila leaf directly to a burn." (True)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Ginger is good for infections." (True)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Match the plant name to its described use based on the audio. (e.g., Sábila -> Soothes burns; Mint leaves -> Relieves headaches; Ginger -> Treats infections)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time]</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Infinitive of purpose (used to + infinitive verb), Should/shouldn't for advice, Present simple for facts.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> plant, leaf, root, remedy, ginger, infections, treatment, herb, application, soursop, aloe vera, lemongrass, mint leaves; treat, heal, soothe, apply, grow, boil, prepare; useful, medicinal, natural, therapeutic; for, with; traditionally, safely.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Compound nouns (e.g., "herbal garden"), syllable division (e.g., "treatment," "therapeutic").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Giving advice on usage, stating purpose.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Listen and Fill):</b> The teacher plays short sentences from the audio, paused after each, or reads new sentences using the target language. Students listen and complete gap-fill sentences on a worksheet. For example: "Sábila is _____ to soothe burns." (used); "You _____ apply it directly." (should); "Mint leaves are _____ for headaches." (good). (<i>Listening Focus: Students listen for specific words and grammatical structures to complete sentences accurately.</i>)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice (Listen and Categorize):</b> The teacher plays an audio clip of descriptions of different healing plants and their uses (e.g., "Guanábana is a fruit. It's used to treat infections and improve digestion."). Students listen and fill in a table with columns: "Plant Name," "Part Used," "Problem Treated," "How to Apply." (<i>Listening Focus: Students listen for multiple pieces of specific information and organize it receptively.</i>)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction / Peer Check (Listen, Dictate & Clarify):</b> In pairs, Student A listens as the teacher dictates a sentence about a remedy (e.g., "Lemongrass leaves are used to help with colds. You should boil them to prepare a tea."). Student A then dictates the sentence to Student B, who writes it down. Student B must listen carefully and can ask, "Can you repeat how to use the plant?" or "How do you spell 'lemongrass'?" Student A clarifies. They compare with the original sentence from the teacher. They switch roles. (<i>Listening Focus: Students practice receptive listening, interactive listening for clarification, and pronunciation of target vocabulary.</i>)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time]</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 10px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "The Healing Plants of Panama".</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Herbal Remedy Advice Exchange" - Paired role-play.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a card with a common minor ailment (e.g., "minor burn," "headache," "upset stomach," "sore throat"). One student describes their ailment using simple sentences (e.g., "I have a small burn on my arm."). The other student listens carefully and provides advice on a suitable healing plant remedy using the structures "You should..." and "It's used to...". The first student must then ask for clarification if needed (e.g., "How do I prepare it?" or "Can you repeat how to use the plant?"). They then switch roles.
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Example Dialogue:</b>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "Oh no, I have a small burn on my finger."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: (<i>Listening to the problem</i>) "You should use sábila (aloe vera). It's used to soothe burns and promote healing."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: (<i>Listening to the advice</i>) "How do I prepare it?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "You should apply the gel from the leaf directly to the skin, traditionally."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "Can you repeat how to use the plant? Just the application part."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "Yes, you just apply the gel safely to the burn."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
                (<i>Listening Focus: Students actively listen to understand ailments and advice, and practice interactive listening by asking for and giving clarification.</i>)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time]</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Herbal Remedy Advice Exchange." The teacher circulates, noting students' ability to listen, provide relevant advice, and ask/answer clarification questions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate actively in the pair role-play, demonstrating receptive and interactive listening skills by understanding the ailment, providing appropriate advice, and clarifying instructions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listening Comprehension:</b> Ability to follow spoken explanations of ailments and remedies.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Interactive Listening:</b> Ability to ask for and provide clarification (e.g., "Can you repeat how to use the plant?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Language:</b> Correct application of 'should/shouldn't' and 'used to' phrases, and use of relevant vocabulary (e.g., plant, remedy, soothe, apply, aloe vera, mint leaves, treatment).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Intelligible pronunciation of key vocabulary, including compound nouns.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Active participation and respectful turn-taking in the pair interaction.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a short self-reflection:
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can understand advice about healing plants for common problems: (circle one) Strongly Agree / Agree / Neutral / Disagree"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can ask my partner to repeat or explain when I don't understand: (circle one) Always / Sometimes / Rarely"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time]</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most interesting healing plant or remedy you learned about today, and what is it used for?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did listening carefully help you understand the different ways to use healing plants?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did the students successfully follow extended speech on familiar topics, particularly the audio input? Were the audio materials engaging and appropriately leveled for A2.2?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How well did students ensure mutual understanding in group interactions through clarification and confirmation? Were there specific phrases they struggled with?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What specific vocabulary items (e.g., 'therapeutic,' 'application') or grammatical structures (e.g., infinitive of purpose) need more explicit reinforcement or varied practice in the next lesson?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What adjustments can I make to the listening tasks or scaffolding for students who found it challenging to follow the explanations?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "In our next lesson, we will reinforce our understanding of 'The Healing Plants of Panama' by reading a text that describes these same plants and their uses, building on the vocabulary and concepts we practiced today. We will focus on reading comprehension strategies."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time]</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>